  Level 1 — Concept 3: Operators in TypeScript
Operators ka use calculations karne, values compare karne aur decisions lene ke liye hota hai.
Example: Tum ek shopping application bana rahe ho. Product price ₹500 hai aur discount ₹100 hai. Final price calculate karne ke liye operator use karoge.
Aaj hum 4 types ke operators seekhenge:
1. Arithmetic operators
2. Comparison operators
3. Logical operators
4. Assignment operators
1. Arithmetic Operators — calculations
Ye maths ke operators hain.
Operator	Meaning	Example	Result
+	Addition	10 + 5	15
-	Subtraction	10 - 5	5
*	Multiplication	10 * 5	50
/	Division	10 / 5	2
%	Remainder	10 % 3	1
**	Power	2 ** 3	8
Code khud type karo
let a: number = 10;
let b: number = 3;

console.log(a + b);
console.log(a - b);
console.log(a * b);
console.log(a / b);
console.log(a % b);
console.log(a ** b);


Output:
13
7
30
3.3333333333333335
1
1000


Important: % operator
10 % 3 ka result 1 hai, kyunki 10 ko 3 se divide karne par remainder 1 bachta hai.
Ye even/odd check karne mein bahut useful hai.
let number: number = 7;

console.log(number % 2);


Output 1 aayega. Agar remainder 0 hai, toh number even hai.
2. Comparison Operators — values compare karna
Ye operators comparison ka result true ya false mein dete hain.
Operator	Meaning
===	Equal value aur equal type
!==	Value ya type mein difference
>	Greater than
<	Less than
>=	Greater than or equal
<=	Less than or equal
Example:
let age: number = 24;

console.log(age > 18);
console.log(age < 18);
console.log(age === 24);
console.log(age !== 24);


Output:
true
false
true
false


== aur === mein difference
JavaScript/TypeScript mein:
console.log(5 == "5");  // true
console.log(5 === "5"); // false


== type conversion kar sakta hai, jabki === type conversion kiye bina compare karta hai.
Professional rule: Generally === aur !== prefer karo.
