Ab hum Functions seekhenge. Functions programming ka bahut important concept hai — real projects mein hum baar-baar functions use karte hain.
1. Function kya hota hai?
Function ek aisa code block hai jise hum jab chahein call karke chala sakte hain.
Maan lo tumhe 3 baar greeting print karni hai.
Bina function ke:
console.log("Hello Rahul");
console.log("Hello Rahul");
console.log("Hello Rahul");


Function ke saath:
function greet() {
    console.log("Hello Rahul");
}

greet();
greet();
greet();


Output:
Hello Rahul
Hello Rahul
Hello Rahul


Yaad rakho:
- function → function banata hai.
- greet → function ka naam hai.
- { } → function ka code.
- greet() → function ko call karta hai.
Function banana aur usko call karna alag cheezein hain.
2. Parameters — function ko data dena
Maan lo har baar alag naam se greeting karni hai.
function greet(name: string) {
    console.log("Hello " + name);
}

greet("Rahul");
greet("Amit");
greet("Priya");


Output:
Hello Rahul
Hello Amit
Hello Priya


Yahan name: string ek parameter hai. "Rahul" ek argument hai jo hum function call karte waqt dete hain.
Ek aur example:
function add(a: number, b: number) {
    console.log(a + b);
}

add(10, 20);
add(5, 3);


Output:
30
8


TypeScript mein a aur b dono numbers hone chahiye.
3. Return — function se result wapas lena
console.log() sirf value print karta hai. return result ko function se bahar wapas deta hai, jise hum aage use kar sakte hain.
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
    return a + b;
}

let result: number = add(10, 20);

console.log(result);
console.log(result * 2);


Output:
30
60


Is line ko samjho:
function add(a: number, b: number): number


Aakhri : number ka matlab hai function ek number return karega.
Memory trick:
- console.log() = screen par dikhao.
- return = result wapas do.
