    age: 22
};

console.log(student.name);
console.log(student.age);


Output:
Rahul
22


Method 2: Bracket notation
console.log(student["name"]);
console.log(student["age"]);


Output same hoga.
Trick: Normally student.name use karna sabse simple hai.
3. Object ki value change karna
let student = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 22
};

student.age = 23;

console.log(student.age);


Output:
23


let se banaye object ki property change kar sakte ho. const se banaye object ki properties bhi generally change ki ja sakti hain, lekin us variable ko kisi naye object se reassign nahi kar sakte.
4. TypeScript mein object ka type define karna
Ab important TypeScript concept dekho.
let employee: {
    name: string;
    age: number;
    isActive: boolean;
} = {
    name: "Amit",
    age: 25,
    isActive: true
};

console.log(employee.name);


Yahan humne object ka structure define kiya hai:
- name string hona chahiye.
- age number hona chahiye.
- isActive boolean hona chahiye.
Agar age: "twenty-five" likhoge, to TypeScript type error dega, kyunki age ke liye number chahiye.
5. Optional property
Kabhi-kabhi object ki property hona zaroori nahi hota. Uske liye ? use karte hain.
let user: {
    name: string;
    email?: string;
} = {
    name: "Rahul"
};

console.log(user.name);
console.log(user.email);


Output:
Rahul
undefined


email? ka matlab email dena optional hai.

